if whatever reason you can only get one  weight to train with say at home then I  recommend going with 25 to 30 of your  body weight because the perfect weight  for you is going to depend a lot on your  specific goals however this is a great  place to start particularly for  explosiveness and athleticism but it can  also do a great job in those other areas  as well so studies show that if you want  to improve your explosiveness then going  up to 30 of body weight is the highest  upper limit reason being because you  have to go slower to lift more weight  than that meaning you're grinding out  movements and not being explosive and  powerful and fast think of it this way  25 to 30 of body weight is the perfect  amount to progressively overload your  own body weight of course for that  explosiveness we're talking specifically  about explosive compound movements  things like a weighted jump squat for  example alternatively though if we're  talking about isolation movements then  this kind of weight is actually quite  good for offering a significant  challenge for things like dumbbell curls  and for compound movements that aren't  performed explosively this is actually  really good for building strength  endurance and this is really important  because how often do you need to lift  something really heavy once in life  versus lifting something slightly  lighter an awful lot of time
 

